  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Light showers and light drizzle will continue \nto dissipate through the evening. Patchy fog possible overnight \nprior to cold frontal passage. Turning drier Monday, then much\ncooler Monday night.\n\nLight showers and some light drizzle across portions of PA and\nNJ will continue to gradually diminish through the remainder of\nthe evening as a weak low moves offshore. Clouds and perhaps \nsome patchy fog or drizzle will linger overnight before a cold\nfront passes through and high pressure builds in from the west.\nThis will dry things out by Monday morning. Overnight\ntemperatures will be mainly in the 50s.\n\nOn Monday, a modest northwest pressure gradient will be in place\nas the high pressure builds into the area. This will create\nbreezy conditions, with northwest winds increasing to 10-15 mph\nwith gusts of 20-30 mph through much of the day. Temperatures\nwill remain mild on Monday, with highs in the upper 60s to low\n70s in most areas as the cooler airmass lags slightly behind the\npressure gradient and downsloping gives us an adiabatic warming\nboost.\n\nMonday night will be by far the coolest so far this season, as\ntemperatures fall into the 40s in many areas. Coastal areas and\nthe Philly urban core will likely stay closer to 50 degrees, and\nareas north of I-78 will drop into the mid to upper 30s in some\nspots. There could be some patchy frost in the sheltered valleys\nof NW NJ and the southern Poconos, but confidence on this is\nsomewhat low give the gradient wind that will still be in place.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Tranquil weather expected this week with no \nsignificant weather hazards anticipated. \n\nHigh pressure will be in control through much of the week resulting \nin mostly clear skies and comfortable temperatures. We will feel a \nbit of an early Fall chill with a cold front passing early \nMonday though. Highs on Tuesday only get into the low to mid 60s\nwith overnight lows on Tuesday Night/Wednesday morning dropping\ninto the upper 30s and low to mid 40s. Even with the early Fall\nchill, temperatures should remain high enough to not need any \nfrost headlines just yet. Cannot rule out some temperatures in \nthe mid 30s though in the Poconos. \n\nTemperatures return to more seasonable levels on Wednesday and \nlikely go into the above normal range towards the end of the week as \nit should be a pleasant stretch of days.",
